{
  "abstract": "Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of the preprint \"A narrow band method for non-causal discretizations of anisotropic eikonal equations, with adaptive width, sub- and super-solution construction, and quasi-linear complexity\" (J.-M. Mirebeau, Y. Mouheb, 2026). The preprint introduces the adaptive narrow band (ANB) method, which solves the fixed point problem Λu = u of a monotone, possibly non-causal, discretization Λ of a Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman or Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation, such as the anisotropic eikonal equation, up to a relative tolerance ε, by constructing a sub-solution and a super-solution. The compared theorems concern the algorithm meant to be implemented, the ANB with sorted lists: when the scheme is sub-additive in Kruzkov exponential coordinates, the fixed point exists, is unique and satisfies a comparison principle, and the algorithm terminates with a sub-solution and a super-solution within relative distance ε, after O(|ln ε|²) updates at each point uniformly in the discretization, with explicit bounds on the number of iterations, the width of the narrow band, the number of evaluations of the causal operator and the number of operations on the sorted lists. These bounds yield the quasi-linear complexity O(N |ln ε| ln(N/ε)) of the paper. The development proves all the lemmas, propositions and theorems of the preprint (151 statements), including the correctness of the generalized Dijkstra method with a stopping time and the equivalence of the ANB with its implementation by sorted lists.",
